module contract;

// mpack writer primitives — resource-projection vocabulary version 1.
//
// Card W37-CORPUS-CONTRACTS-4. FOUR TRUSTED ASSUMPTIONS about four upstream
// functions. This module verifies nothing: the C bodies are not analyzed by
// this loader, and a declaration is a premise. Any verdict that rests on one is
// a verdict RELATIVE TO this declaration, never a proof of the C.
//
// Read from the pinned checkout (`build/cycle19/c-gateway-wave-pin/
// fluent-bit-clang-source`) at Fluent Bit
// 66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97 on this card's own run.
//
// ## This is a NEW contract family, and it is NOT `msgpack/`
//
// `contract/qcore/msgpack/packer.qcore` declares `msgpack_packer_init` of
// **msgpack-c**. The functions below belong to **mpack**, a different library
// with a different API (`mpack_writer_t` vs `msgpack_packer`). The two are
// vendored side by side in this tree and share only a naming resemblance, so
// they get separate families; merging them would be a naming error, not a
// tidy-up. `W37-MPACK-FAMILY-FIRST` records that the directory name and its
// manifest position are this card's choice alone.
//
// ## Four vendored copies, and why that is NOT the `ADD-NAME-COLLISION` case
//
// A resource contract is keyed by NAME, so a name with several definitions in
// the tree is a hazard — card W36-CORPUS-CONTRACTS-3 declined `add` for exactly
// that reason (three definitions, three DIFFERENT signatures, one of them
// `static inline` with no header declaration at all).
//
// Each of the four names below has **four** definitions, and the four were
// compared rather than assumed:
//
//     lib/mpack-amalgamation-1.1.1/src/mpack/mpack.c   i64:1988 double:2038 str:2202 cstr:2279
//     lib/cmetrics/lib/mpack/src/mpack/mpack.c         i64:1774 double:1816 str:1968 cstr:2045
//     lib/ctraces/lib/mpack/src/mpack/mpack.c          i64:1774 double:1816 str:1968 cstr:2045
//     lib/cprofiles/lib/mpack/src/mpack/mpack.c        i64:1774 double:1816 str:1968 cstr:2045
//
// and all four copies spell each signature IDENTICALLY, character for
// character, with external linkage and a matching header prototype in each
// copy's own `mpack.h`. These are four vendored copies of ONE library at one
// version, not four different functions sharing a name. A declaration true of
// all four is therefore not the `add` error: the distinction that matters is
// not "how many definitions" but "do they agree", and here they do — measured.
//
// ## Why these callees
//
// Chosen by FUNCTION-COVER yield over the baseline's 163 projected functions
// whose only refusals are `call to uncontracted function` leaves. Each of the
// four is the SOLE blocker of exactly one projected function:
//
//   * `mpack_write_cstr`  -> `pack_cmetrics_type`
//                            (`lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_encode_cloudwatch_emf.c:93-118`,
//                             eight leaves, one per call)
//   * `mpack_write_double`-> `pack_double` (`lib/ctraces/src/ctr_encode_msgpack.c:45-48`)
//   * `mpack_write_i64`   -> `pack_int64`  (`lib/ctraces/src/ctr_encode_msgpack.c:40-43`)
//   * `mpack_write_str`   -> `pack_string` (`lib/ctraces/src/ctr_encode_msgpack.c:35-38`)
//
// ## The bodies, read before declaring
//
// `mpack_write_double` (cmetrics copy, `mpack.c:1816-1819`) is the whole shape:
//
//     void mpack_write_double(mpack_writer_t* writer, double value) {
//         mpack_writer_track_element(writer);
//         MPACK_WRITE_ENCODED(mpack_encode_double, MPACK_TAG_SIZE_DOUBLE, value);
//     }
//
// `mpack_write_i64` (`:1774`) is the same with an integer encoding and a
// size-optimised delegation to `mpack_write_u64` for values above 127.
//
// `mpack_write_str` (`:1968`) writes a length tag and then copies `count`
// bytes out of `data` into the writer's buffer (`mpack_memcpy(p + ..., data,
// count)`), growing that buffer through `mpack_writer_ensure` when needed.
//
// `mpack_write_cstr` (`:2045-2051`):
//
//     void mpack_write_cstr(mpack_writer_t* writer, const char* cstr) {
//         mpack_assert(cstr != NULL, "cstr pointer is NULL");
//         size_t length = mpack_strlen(cstr);
//         if (length > UINT32_MAX)
//             mpack_writer_flag_error(writer, mpack_error_invalid);
//         mpack_write_str(writer, cstr, (uint32_t)length);
//     }
//
//   * `writer` is the output context. Every one of the four READS and WRITES
//     THROUGH it and none of them frees it, stores it anywhere that outlives
//     the call, or transfers it. The caller's obligation — to
//     `mpack_writer_destroy` the writer it initialised — is exactly what it was
//     before the call, which is what `resource_borrowed` says.
//   * `data`/`cstr` are `const char *`. They are read (`mpack_strlen`,
//     `mpack_memcpy`) and never freed, never stored, never returned. Borrowed.
//   * `value`/`count` are scalars passed BY VALUE. They are not pointers, carry
//     no custody, and are declared borrowed for the same reason `mk_print`'s
//     `level` is.
//   * every result is `void`, so `resource_return_none()` is the faithful — and
//     the only faithful — result mode.
//
// ## NOT CLAIMED, recorded rather than hidden
//
//   * **The writer's INTERNAL buffer may be reallocated.** Under
//     `mpack_writer_init_growable` the flush callback reallocs
//     `writer->buffer`, so a byte address inside the writer is not stable
//     across these calls. That is a fact about a FIELD of the borrowed object,
//     not about the parameter's custody: the caller holds the `mpack_writer_t
//     *`, that pointer is unchanged, and the caller's destroy obligation is
//     unchanged. This projection models parameter custody only and says
//     nothing about interior addresses (`MPACK-WRITER-INTERIOR-REALLOC`).
//   * Nothing about the encoding being well-formed msgpack, about the writer's
//     error flag, about whether a flush succeeded, or about `count` matching
//     `data`'s real length. `mpack_write_str` with a `count` larger than the
//     buffer is an upstream out-of-bounds read this declaration neither creates
//     nor detects; a custody projection models custody.
//   * `mpack_assert(cstr != NULL, ...)` compiles out in the pinned
//     configuration, and no non-nullness is asserted here in any case: v1
//     carries a nullability marker only on a RELEASED parameter, and nothing
//     below releases anything.
//   * Nothing about thread safety. Two threads writing one writer is a data
//     race this projection neither models nor excludes.
//
// ## ABI and modes (constraint 8)
//
// Header prototypes (cmetrics copy, `lib/cmetrics/lib/mpack/src/mpack/mpack.h`;
// the other three copies agree):
//
//     void mpack_write_i64(mpack_writer_t* writer, int64_t value);        // :2787
//     void mpack_write_double(mpack_writer_t* writer, double value);      // :2824
//     void mpack_write_str(mpack_writer_t* writer, const char* str,
//                          uint32_t length);                              // :2958
//     void mpack_write_cstr(mpack_writer_t* writer, const char* cstr);    // :2984
//
// `mpack_writer_t *` is one object-pointer level, `ptr<mut, u8>`; `const char
// *` is one object-pointer level, `ptr<const, u8>`. Under the LP64 model of the
// audited targets (`LOADER-ABI-LP64`) `int64_t` projects to `i64`, `uint32_t`
// to `u32`, `double` to `f64`, and the `void` results to `unit`. Arities are
// two, two, three and two, as declared.
//
// `mpack_write_str`'s header spells its parameters `str, length` where its
// definition spells them `data, count`. A declaration's parameter NAMES are its
// own — the ABI comparison is over types, pointer levels and arity — so this
// module follows the DEFINITION's spelling, and the disagreement is recorded
// here rather than silently resolved.
//
// ## Defined in no analysed unit
//
// None of the four mpack `.c` copies is among the 185 analysed units, so
// constraint 8's duty gate has no derived summary to compare against and
// nothing here can be withdrawn on a body this corpus does not define
// ("absence of evidence is not evidence"). This was checked against the
// baseline's own unit list, not assumed.
//
// ## Read at the same time and DELIBERATELY LEFT UNDECLARED
//
// `mpack_writer_track_element`, `mpack_writer_ensure`, `mpack_write_u64`,
// `mpack_strlen`, `mpack_memcpy`, `mpack_writer_flag_error`,
// `mpack_start_str_notrack` and `mpack_write_native` are all named by these
// bodies and none is declared: this card selects by function-cover yield and
// none of them is the sole blocker of any projected function in the baseline.
// `mpack_write_cstr_or_nil` (`:2053`) is the nullable sibling of
// `mpack_write_cstr` and blocks nothing.

// --- a NUL-terminated string written as an msgpack str ---
extern fn mpack_write_cstr(writer: ptr<mut, u8>, cstr: ptr<const, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(cstr);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- a counted byte range written as an msgpack str ---
extern fn mpack_write_str(writer: ptr<mut, u8>, data: ptr<const, u8>, count: u32) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(data);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(count);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- a signed 64-bit integer written as an msgpack int ---
extern fn mpack_write_i64(writer: ptr<mut, u8>, value: i64) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(value);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- a double written as an msgpack float64 ---
extern fn mpack_write_double(writer: ptr<mut, u8>, value: f64) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(value);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// ## Card W46-EXTERNAL-CALLEE-CONTRACTS: five more writer primitives
//
// FIVE TRUSTED ASSUMPTIONS, pinned (contract spec C06.2) after reading every
// vendored copy at both audited revisions. Chosen because, with `cfl_sds_len`
// (`cfl/sds_lifetime.qcore`), they are the last uncontracted calls of three
// projected functions: `pack_basic_header_finish`
// (`lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_encode_cloudwatch_emf.c:86-91`, `mpack_finish_map`
// and `mpack_finish_array`), `pack_bool`
// (`lib/ctraces/src/ctr_encode_msgpack.c:25-33`, `mpack_write_true` and
// `mpack_write_false`) and `pack_bytes` (`:88-96`, `mpack_write_bin`). The
// other callers of these names keep other blockers and only lose leaves.
//
// ## The bodies, read before declaring
//
// cmetrics, ctraces and cprofiles copies (`mpack.h:2918-2934`, `mpack.c:1406`,
// `:1410`, `:2023`; the three are identical):
//
//     MPACK_INLINE void mpack_finish_array(mpack_writer_t* writer) {
//         mpack_writer_track_pop(writer, mpack_type_array);
//     }
//     MPACK_INLINE void mpack_finish_map(mpack_writer_t* writer) {
//         mpack_writer_track_pop(writer, mpack_type_map);
//     }
//     void mpack_write_true(mpack_writer_t* writer) {
//         mpack_write_byte_element(writer, (char)0xc3);
//     }
//     void mpack_write_false(mpack_writer_t* writer) {
//         mpack_write_byte_element(writer, (char)0xc2);
//     }
//     void mpack_write_bin(mpack_writer_t* writer, const char* data, uint32_t count) {
//         mpack_assert(data != NULL, "data pointer for bin of %i bytes is NULL", (int)count);
//         mpack_start_bin(writer, count);
//         mpack_write_bytes(writer, data, count);
//         mpack_finish_bin(writer);
//     }
//
// `mpack_writer_track_pop` is a no-op (`MPACK_UNUSED` of both arguments)
// unless `MPACK_WRITE_TRACKING` is on, which `mpack.h:283-288` enables only in
// an `MPACK_DEBUG` build; there it pops the writer's own tracking stack. Either
// way only state inside the writer changes. The amalgamation copy
// (`lib/mpack-amalgamation-1.1.1/src/mpack/mpack.h:3759`, `:3775`) adds
// `mpack_builder_compound_pop(writer)` to both finishers, which closes a
// builder compound held inside the writer; its `mpack_write_bin` asserts
// `count == 0 || data != NULL` instead. The bodies therefore DIFFER between
// copies, and the declarations are nevertheless true of every copy: no copy
// frees, stores or transfers any argument. This is the "do they agree on the
// custody effect" test of the module header, not a claim of equal bodies, and
// the pin records each copy's own body digest.
//
//   * `writer` is read and written through (the tag byte, the builder or
//     tracking state, the buffer) and nothing else happens to it: the caller's
//     obligation to destroy the writer is unchanged. `resource_borrowed`.
//   * `data` is copied into the writer's buffer by `mpack_write_bytes` and is
//     never freed, stored or returned. `resource_borrowed`.
//   * `count` is a scalar by value. `resource_borrowed`.
//   * every result is `void`: `resource_return_none()`.
//
// NOT CLAIMED, as for the four declarations above: the writer's internal
// buffer may be flushed or reallocated (`MPACK-WRITER-INTERIOR-REALLOC`);
// nothing about the encoding, the error flag, balanced start/finish pairs, or
// `count` matching `data`'s length.
//
// ## ABI (constraint 8)
//
// Header prototypes, identical in the three library copies (`mpack.h:2830`,
// `:2833`, `:3037`) and in the amalgamation (`:3639`, `:3642`, `:3955`):
//
//     void mpack_write_true(mpack_writer_t* writer);
//     void mpack_write_false(mpack_writer_t* writer);
//     void mpack_write_bin(mpack_writer_t* writer, const char* data, uint32_t count);
//
// The finishers are defined in the header with `MPACK_INLINE`. No `.c` copy is
// an analysed unit and no analysed function is one of these names, so no body
// in the corpus is checked against these declarations and nothing is withdrawn
// on one.

// --- close a map (a no-op or a builder pop; see above) ---
extern fn mpack_finish_map(writer: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- close an array (a no-op or a builder pop; see above) ---
extern fn mpack_finish_array(writer: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- the msgpack `true` byte ---
extern fn mpack_write_true(writer: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- the msgpack `false` byte ---
extern fn mpack_write_false(writer: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- a counted byte range written as an msgpack bin ---
extern fn mpack_write_bin(writer: ptr<mut, u8>, data: ptr<const, u8>, count: u32) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(data);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(count);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// # Card CFL-MPACK-EXTERNAL-DECLARATIONS (round 14, 2026-11-05)
//
// USER REQUEST 2026-11-05 (verbatim): "Now, we're still needed to fill the
// gap of the missing contracts." The user's `quirin-audit current` run at
// 046accf6 reports these mpack callees as uncontracted. Each declaration
// below is NEW resource-v1 text read from the callee's body. None is adopted
// from another vocabulary (card CONTRACT-ADOPTION-NOT-IN-FORCE §5).
//
// The bodies were read in the cmetrics copy and compared with the others.
// The cmetrics, ctraces and cprofiles copies of `mpack.c`/`mpack.h` are
// byte-identical to one another and across 66910c10, 046accf6 and the slice
// export 34155955. The amalgamation copy (`lib/mpack-amalgamation-1.1.1`) was
// read for every name it defines. Fluent Bit's own units (`flb_mp.c`,
// `cm_utils.c`) include it. Line numbers are the cmetrics copy's at 046accf6.
// The census is `docs/reports/cfl-mpack-external-declarations/`. The reader
// primitives sit in this module, the crate's one selected module, so that
// the selection manifest, the load path and the module counts do not move.
//
// ## The error state, stated
//
// Every reader and writer primitive below may set the object's error. A
// writer does so through `mpack_writer_flag_error` (`mpack.c:1214-1222`):
// after a failed growth (`mpack_writer_ensure`, `:1256-1284`, and the
// growable flush's `realloc`, `:1072-1076`), a writer without a flush
// function, or a tracking error. A reader does so through
// `mpack_reader_flag_error` (`:2280-2289`): after truncated input with no
// fill function (`:2387-2391`, `:2330-2333`), an unexpected type, or a
// string longer than its buffer. Setting the error does the following and
// nothing else:
//
//   * It stores the error code in the object, once (a set error is never
//     overwritten).
//   * For a reader, it also clamps `reader->end` to `reader->data`, so every
//     later read sees no data, zero-fills its output and returns.
//   * For a writer, every later write is a no-op.
//   * It calls the object's `error_fn` callback when one is installed.
//
// Nothing the caller holds is allocated, freed, stored or moved by it. The
// writer's buffer stays the writer's: `mpack_writer_destroy`'s growable
// teardown (`:1090-1125`) frees it on an error and hands it to the
// initialiser's target only on success. That custody effect belongs to
// `mpack_writer_init_growable`/`mpack_writer_destroy`, which stay
// undeclared (below). The error state is therefore custody-neutral at every
// primitive here, and the declarations carry it as `resource_borrowed` of
// the object.
//
// ## The callbacks the bodies can reach
//
// A writer calls `writer->flush`, `writer->error_fn` and (at destroy)
// `writer->teardown`. A reader calls `reader->fill`, `reader->skip`,
// `reader->error_fn` and (at destroy) `reader->teardown`. These are fields
// of the borrowed object, installed only by the `mpack_*_set_*` setters and
// the stream/file initialisers. The census (`census/bodies.py`) finds no call
// of any of those in Fluent Bit's `src`, `include` and `plugins`, or in the
// cmetrics, ctraces and cprofiles sources, at any of the three revisions.
// The audited code initialises writers only with `mpack_writer_init_growable`
// (flush and teardown: the growable pair above, which touch the writer's own
// buffer and the initialiser's target) and readers only with
// `mpack_reader_init_data` (no callback). The declarations are true of
// objects so initialised. An object carrying a user callback is outside what
// they state. No premise names this: v1's premises are metadata. The
// boundary joins W46-METRIC-TRACE-CALLBACK-PREMISES, which already names
// the mpack fill and error callbacks.
//
// ## Tracking is off in every audited unit
//
// `MPACK_READ_TRACKING`/`MPACK_WRITE_TRACKING` follow `MPACK_DEBUG`, which
// follows `DEBUG`/`_DEBUG` (`mpack.h:225-288`). No audited compile database
// defines either for a unit that includes mpack. The census lists the one
// unit that does, `lib/lwrb/dev/main.c`. So the tracking pushes, pops and
// destroys compile to nothing. Where tracking is on, they grow or free a
// stack inside the object, which is still the object's own state.

// ## More writer primitives
//
//   * `mpack_start_map(writer, count)` and `mpack_start_array(writer, count)`
//     (`:1863-1875`, `:1849-1861`) write a map or array header, sized by
//     `count`, into the writer's buffer, and push a tracking frame. The
//     amalgamation copy (`:2104-2109`, `:2097-2102`) also pushes a builder
//     compound. A builder page is `MPACK_MALLOC`ed into the writer's own
//     builder list (`:2446-2461`) and is the writer's state.
//   * `mpack_write_nil` (`:1398-1400`), `mpack_write_u32` (`:1679-1694`),
//     `mpack_write_u64` (`:1696-1710`) and `mpack_write_i32` (`:1748-1772`)
//     encode one value into the writer's buffer, which may grow (see
//     `MPACK-WRITER-INTERIOR-REALLOC`). The amalgamation copies (`:1598`,
//     `:1893`, `:1910`, `:1962`) differ only in their constant names.
//   * `mpack_write_int` and `mpack_write_uint` are `MPACK_INLINE` header
//     definitions (`mpack.h:2790-2792`, `:2807-2809`; amalgamation `:3589`,
//     `:3606`) that call `mpack_write_i64` (declared above) and
//     `mpack_write_u64`. They are of external linkage, so no unit's body is
//     admitted for them (W44-CALLEE-EXTERNAL-INLINE-DEFINITION). This
//     declaration is that entry's second discharge arm for these two names.
//   * `mpack_writer_flag_error(writer, error)` (`:1214-1222`; amalgamation
//     `:1317-1325`) is the error-state setter described above.
//
// `writer` is `resource_borrowed`; every scalar is `resource_borrowed`; every
// result is `void`. Prototypes `mpack.h:2738`, `:2784`, `:2801`, `:2804`,
// `:2836`, `:2892`, `:2906`. `mpack_error_t` is an enumeration with no
// negative constant (`:1380-1391`), so it projects to `u32` (rule 12u).
// The gateway asks Clang for that type in each unit. `int32_t` is `i32`,
// `int64_t` `i64`, `uint32_t` `u32`, `uint64_t` `u64`.
//
// LEFT UNDECLARED: `mpack_writer_init_growable(writer, target_data,
// target_size)` (`:1127-1151`). It stores the caller's two slots in the
// writer (`growable_writer->target_data = target_data`, `:1138`) and
// `MPACK_MALLOC`s the buffer. `mpack_writer_destroy` later writes an owned
// buffer through the stored slot, on a non-error state only (`:1115`). This
// is a retention of a depth-two slot (C04.14 admits depth-one pointers only)
// and a deferred, status-conditioned out slot. v1 has neither. Follow-up:
// `MPACK-GROWABLE-WRITER-DEFERRED-OUT-SLOT`.

extern fn mpack_start_map(writer: ptr<mut, u8>, count: u32) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(writer);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(count);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

